 africa-fellows-graduate-student-showcase\n報名/票券: https://forms.gle
 /YDkeva69naPKpfpe8\n\nSesssion 1: \n\nKampala is a Swamp: Power\, Place\, 
 and Devotion\, 1900–2025\n\nToday more than seventy percent of Kampala
 ’s residents live in informal settlements built in the city’s wetlands
 \, yet the historiography of Uganda’s capital has been written almost en
 tirely from its hills. This dissertation rewrites Kampala’s history from
  its lowlands\, arguing that the wetlands of Central Uganda are not margin
 al to the regions past but instead represent the terrain on which competin
 g claims to the city have been made and contested for the past 125 years. 
 Kampala is a Swamp traces two entwined histories. The first is a vocabular
 y of environmental rule I call the “swamp lexicon.” Beginning with the
  sleeping sickness epidemic that emptied the Ssese Islands after 1903\, Br
 itish officials and their Baganda allies translated anxieties about diseas
 e\, race\, and disorder into policies of drainage\, eviction\, and segrega
 ted urban planning casting wetlands as space that required close control. 
 The racialized urban planning that followed left swamps as some of the onl
 y land available for African residents who built wetland neighborhoods tha
 t continue to define Kampala to this day. When the National Resistance Mov
 ement took power in 1986 and adopted some of the world’s most progressiv
 e wetland conservation laws\, they supplied fresh grounds for evicting com
 munities from wetland neighborhoods. The second history belongs to a group
  of residents I call “sacred citizens.” Through devotion to wetland sp
 irits
